      "year": 1999,
      "abstract": "Alexander M. Martin. Romantics, Reformers, Reactionaries: Russian Conservative Thought and Politics in the Reign of Alexander I. DeKalb, IL: Northern Illinois University Press, 1997. x, 294 pp. Illustrations. Notes. Bibliography. Index. $35.00, cloth. Soviet scholars largely demonized or ignored Russian conservative thought and thinkers of the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Their reception outside the Soviet Union, however, was hardly better. Interest in Russian letters has tended to look more towards the progressive or liberal aspects of Russian history and literature, than towards backward-looking conservatism or reactionary ideas. To an extent, this is changing. The conservative side of Russian history is attracting more sympathetic interest. Albeit not the first, Martin's study is indicative of this trend, which explores creative aspects of Russian conservative thought and its place in Russian life and development. Other works on conservatism in Russia during the end of the eighteenth century and the beginning of the nineteenth century that have appeared since Martin's monograph include, for example, D. V. Ermaskov's and A. A. Shiriniants' U Istokov Rossiiskogo Konservatizma: N. M. Karamzin (Moscow, 1999), and A. M. Peskov's Pavel I (Moscow, 1999). For Martin, Russian conservative thought during the reign of Alexander I ( 1802-1825) is \"a multifaceted phenomenon full of contradictions\" (p. 3). It was, moreover, associated with individuals, not with identifiable groups, parties or political movements. This personal dimension determines the approach and focus of his study. Martin states: \"the personal nature of politics and its interaction with public opinion determine the structure of this study... Thus the story of conservatism from 1800 to the 1820s is that of different generations and sociological milieux that are best studied through representative figures who both typified and shaped their collective outlook\" (p. 12). The individuals who occupy the foreground of Martin's study are well known conservative personalities within the reign of Alexander I: Admiral A.S. Shishkov (1754-1841) and S.N. Glinka (1776-1847), whom Martin labels \"romantic nationalists\"; F.V. Rostopchin (1763-1826) and N.M. Karamzin (1766-1826), who are assigned to the category of \"gentry conservatives\"; and A.N. Golitsyn (1773-1844) and the brother-sister team of Roksandra (1786-1844) and Aleksandr (1791-1854) Sturdza, whom Martin designates as \"religious conservatives.\" We also meet a host of supporting figures, such as the poet and statesman G.R. Derzhavin (1743-1816), the Grand Duchess Ekaterina Pavlovna (1788-1818), O.P. Kozodavlev (1754-1819), D.P. Runich (1778-1860) and others. The cast of major players includes literary, military, political and private figures, men and women, from various generations. The large supporting cast adds depth and detail to the cultural milieu of the conservatives and contributes to discussion of the wide differences in conservative thought of the period. Notably missing from the list of major figures is the arch reactionary and confidant of Alexander I, A.A. Arakcheev ( 1769-1834), who receives only minor attention. Martin also passes quickly over M.L. Magnitskii (1778-1855), focussing on his responsibility for purging Kazan' University. While Karamzin fares better, this leading literary figure among the conservative thinkers occupies a secondary role in Martin's study. Martin's work considers the role of conservative figures and the history of Alexander's reign. He categorizes conservative thinkers into three broad groups: romantic nationalists, who \"sought in the uncorrupted culture of the common people an antidote to the moral and political dangers associated with Westernization\" (p. 5); gentry conservatives, who focused on defending the interests of the nobility; and, finally, the religious conservatives, who viewed Russia's development in terms of Christian spirituality. These categories, which often intertwine in the positions taken by individuals, are a useful starting point for exploring the predominant conservative strains of the period. \u2026",
